        "p": "In 1754 a twenty-two-year-old Virginia officer named George Washington marched a few hundred men into a forested valley west of the Appalachian Mountains, looking to push the French out of land his colony claimed. What he set off instead was a war that would burn for nine years across four continents. The shot that started it was fired in a damp Pennsylvania ravine, in an engagement that lasted about fifteen minutes, and the man who turned that skirmish into a killing was not Washington and not the French. He was a Native leader pursuing his own war, for his own reasons, using both empires as his instruments."
